"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[5307],{6302(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Complexity","permalink":"/concepts/tags/complexity","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":3,"items":[{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-analysis","title":"Analyzing Divide and Conquer","description":"The loops of iterative algorithms can be analyzed by counting iterations, as we did in the first chapter. Recursive algorithms need a different tool: the execution time is expressed in terms of itself applied to smaller sizes, giving a recurrence equation. Fortunately, most divide and conquer algorithms follow one of two very regular patterns, and for both of them the solution is known in general. This lesson presents those two patterns, explains where their formulas come from, and shows how to apply them.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-analysis"},{"id":"foundations-of-algorithms/exercises","title":"Exercises: Foundations","description":"These exercises review the whole chapter, from the definition of an algorithm to searching. They are ordered by lesson, and within each group from easier to harder. Try each one before reading its solution: the solutions are grouped at the end of the page.","permalink":"/concepts/foundations-of-algorithms/exercises"},{"id":"advanced-execution-models/parallel-performance","title":"Performance of Parallel Algorithms","description":"A parallel program is only worth its extra complexity if it is faster. But how much faster can it be? Does doubling the number of cores halve the time? This lesson presents the tools to answer these questions: how the complexity of an algorithm relates to its parallel execution time, how to measure the benefit of parallelism, and Amdahl\'s law, which sets a hard limit on it.","permalink":"/concepts/advanced-execution-models/parallel-performance"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);